"""
kaiwa_meaning_en.py

Locked source of truth for the English translations of Kaiwa dialogue
content: KaiwaEntry.titleEn/descriptionEn, KaiwaLine.npcLine.translationEn,
KaiwaAnswerOption.translationEn. apply_kaiwa_meaning_en.py reads this and
patches assets/data/kaiwa_data.json, so translations live in exactly one
place.

Key format (four content kinds share one dict, disambiguated by suffix):
    "{entry_id}|title"              -> the dialogue's title
    "{entry_id}|description"        -> the dialogue's one-line description
    "{entry_id}|{line_id}|npc"      -> an NPC turn's sentence translation
    "{entry_id}|{line_id}|opt{i}"   -> the i-th (0-based) answer option's
                                        translation, for a user turn

line_id is only unique within its own entry (verified dataset-wide: no
entry repeats a line id), so it's always paired with entry_id. opt{i} is
the option's position in its own line's authored options list (stable —
shuffling for display happens at render time in a copy, never in the
data).

Scope note: npcLine.translationEn is not currently rendered anywhere
(KaiwaDialogueScreen shows NPC turns as image + speak button only, no
on-screen text — see CLAUDE.md's Kaiwa section) but is translated anyway
for completeness, since the field exists on the model and may be used
later. option translations ARE rendered today, right after the learner
picks the correct answer.

Must be re-applied after generate_kaiwa_seed.py regenerates the dataset.

STATUS: in progress. Total scope, counted directly from
    assets/data/kaiwa_data.json (1,700 real dialogues, 0 placeholders):
        titles:            1,700
        descriptions:      1,700
        npc lines:         7,468
        answer options:   23,151
        TOTAL:            34,019
    Order: titles + descriptions first (3,400 - smallest, highest
    visibility, shown in every Home/Level/Category list), then answer
    options (23,151 - the actual practice content, rendered after a
    correct tap), then npc lines (7,468 - currently unrendered, lowest
    priority) last. Given the scale, expect this to span many sessions;
    batch and commit incrementally the same way N1 kanji glosses did
    (scripts/kanji_word_meaning_en.py's STATUS block has the full
    history of that approach) so no single interruption loses much work.

    Titles+descriptions: DONE. All 8 batches (3,400/3,400 rows =
    1,700/1,700 entries) committed. Every KaiwaEntry now has both
    titleEn and descriptionEn (verified via full-dataset scan, zero
    entries missing either field).

    Answer options: DONE. All 46 batches (23,151/23,151 rows) committed.
    Every KaiwaAnswerOption now has translationEn (verified via
    apply_kaiwa_meaning_en.py's own per-field coverage printout: opt
    23,151/23,151).

    NPC lines: batches 1-6/? done (3,000/7,468, dump order). Currently
    unrendered anywhere in the app (KaiwaDialogueScreen shows NPC turns
    as image + speak button only), lowest priority, but following the
    same verify-patch-apply-commit cycle used for every batch above.
    Remaining: rows 3,001-7,468 of the npc-lines dump.

    Dump a phase's source rows with, e.g. (titles+descriptions):
        python -c "import json; d=json.load(open('assets/data/kaiwa_data.json', encoding='utf-8')); [print(f\"{e['id']}|title :: {e['title']}\") or print(f\"{e['id']}|description :: {e['description']}\") for e in d]"
    or (answer options):
        python -c "import json; d=json.load(open('assets/data/kaiwa_data.json', encoding='utf-8')); [print(f\"{e['id']}|{l['id']}|opt{i} :: {o['translation']}\") for e in d for l in e['lines'] for i, o in enumerate(l.get('options', []))]"
    or (npc lines):
        python -c "import json; d=json.load(open('assets/data/kaiwa_data.json', encoding='utf-8')); [print(f\"{e['id']}|{l['id']}|npc :: {l['npcLine']['translation']}\") for e in d for l in e['lines'] if l.get('npcLine')]"

Style: translate the Indonesian gloss as it stands - natural
conversational English, no new senses added.
"""

